# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The two parables are read here in the order Jesus tells them. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The treasure (13:44). “Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto treasure hid in a field; the which when a man hath found, he hideth, and for joy thereof goeth and selleth all that he hath, and buyeth that field.” The sequence has seven steps. Treasure is hid in a field. A man finds it. He hides it. He goes “for joy thereof.” He sells all that he has. He buys the field. The story ends with the purchase.'))
A(('p', '3.2 What the treasure story says and does not say. The text says the treasure was “hid” and that the man “hath found” it. It does not say that he was looking for it. It does not say who hid the treasure or why. It does not say why the man hides it again, and it gives no motive for that act. It says the man’s action is “for joy,” and it says he “selleth all that he hath.” It does not say what he gives up beyond “all that he hath,” and it does not say what he does afterward.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The pearl (13:45–46). “Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto a merchant man, seeking goodly pearls: Who, when he had found one pearl of great price, went and sold all that he had, and bought it.” This is a second story with its own sequence. A merchant is seeking goodly pearls. He finds one pearl of great price. He goes and sells all that he has. He buys it.'))
A(('p', '3.4 What the pearl story says and does not say. The text says the merchant was “seeking,” and that what he sought was “goodly pearls” in the plural. It says he “found one pearl of great price.” It does not say where he found it, who owned it, what it cost or what he did with it afterward. It does not mention joy in this story.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The contrasts. The two stories share a movement: finding, selling all, buying. They differ in four things the text shows. The man in the first story “hath found” treasure, and the text does not say he sought it; the merchant in the second was “seeking.” The first story has a field that is bought; the second has no field, and the pearl itself is bought. The first story names joy; the second does not. The first speaks of a hidden thing the man hides; the second speaks of one pearl among “goodly pearls.”'))
A(('p', '3.6 The comparisons themselves. In 13:44 the Kingdom is said to be “like unto treasure hid in a field,” and in 13:45 it is said to be “like unto a merchant man.” The first comparison is to the treasure, and the second is to the man. The text states both comparisons that way and does not explain the difference.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 13:44', '“treasure hid in a field; the which when a man hath found, he hideth”', 'A hidden treasure is found and hidden again.'],
    ['Matt 13:44', '“for joy thereof goeth and selleth all that he hath”', 'The man’s action is done for joy.'],
    ['Matt 13:44', '“buyeth that field”', 'The field is bought, and the story ends.'],
    ['Matt 13:45', '“a merchant man, seeking goodly pearls”', 'The merchant is seeking.'],
    ['Matt 13:46', '“had found one pearl of great price”', 'He finds one pearl of great price.'],
    ['Matt 13:46', '“went and sold all that he had, and bought it”', 'He sells all and buys the pearl itself.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.7 What the stories leave unsaid. Jesus does not say what the treasure is. Jesus does not say what the field is. Jesus does not say who the man is. Jesus does not say what the merchant is, or what the pearl is. Jesus does not say what “all that he hath” includes. The stories give the finding, the joy in the first, the selling and the buying, and they stop.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives no explanation. In Matthew 13 Jesus explains the Sower (13:18–23) and the Wheat and the Weeds (13:36–43). For the hidden treasure and the pearl He gives none. Matthew records no request for an explanation and no answer. This section therefore records no identification, because there is none to record.'))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus does not identify. The parable names some parts of the story and leaves each of them without an explanation. Jesus does not identify the treasure. Jesus does not identify the field. Jesus does not identify the man who finds the treasure. Jesus does not identify the one who hid it. Jesus does not identify the merchant. Jesus does not identify the pearl. Jesus does not identify what “all that he hath” is. Jesus does not identify the selling or the buying as anything beyond what the story says.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of the story', 'What Jesus says it is', 'Verse'],
    ['The treasure', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 13:44'],
    ['The field', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 13:44'],
    ['The man who finds', 'Jesus does not identify him.', 'Matt 13:44'],
    ['The merchant', 'Jesus does not identify him.', 'Matt 13:45'],
    ['The pearl', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 13:45–46'],
    ['“All that he hath”', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 13:44, 46'],
], [0.34, 0.46, 0.20]))
A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the treasure is a certain people, that the pearl is a certain body, that the merchant is a certain person or that the field is a certain place is supplying what the Lord did not. The field of Matthew 13:38 is explained for the Wheat and the Weeds only, and it is not carried into 13:44.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What the text does say. The text says that the Kingdom is like these things, that the treasure was found, that the man acted for joy, that he sold all that he had, and that he bought the field. It says that the merchant was seeking, that he found one pearl of great price, that he sold all that he had, and that he bought it. These statements are made by the stories themselves and are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation is given for these parables, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It assigns no identity to any part of either story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Jesus says the kingdom of heaven is like treasure hid in a field and like a merchant who finds one pearl of great price. Each comparison gives a thing of value, a person who comes upon it, and a response in which the person gives up all he has to gain it. The meaning of the comparison is in what the stories show of the value and of the response.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates about the Kingdom.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The Kingdom is of surpassing worth.** The treasure is worth more than a field’s price and more than all the man has; the pearl is “of great price” and worth more to the merchant than all that he sells to buy it.',
    '**The worth is recognised by the one who finds it.** In each story the finder acts as soon as he finds. The valuation is shown in what he does, and the text does not say that anyone told him.',
    '**The worth can be joyful.** The first story says the man goes “for joy thereof.” The Kingdom is not shown there as a burden taken up with reluctance.',
    '**The worth can be sought or found unsought.** The merchant was seeking, and the text does not say the first man was. The two stories show the Kingdom’s worth meeting a man in both ways.',
    '**The worth outweighs everything held.** Each man sells “all that he hath.” Nothing he owns stands against the gain in his judgment.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers had heard that the Kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17; 10:7). They were meant to understand that the Kingdom they were told of is worth more than all they hold. They were told this in two short pictures, after a day in which the Kingdom had been described as sown, resisted and awaiting harvest. These pictures speak of its value.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the valuation. The force is the force of a reversal of ordinary weights. A field or a pearl is bought at the price of everything else. The parables ask the hearer to weigh what he holds against what the Kingdom is, and to see that the weighing comes out as it did for the man and for the merchant.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. The Kingdom is of surpassing worth. The one who finds its worth acts decisively. What is given up is small beside what is gained. Gladness may accompany the giving. These statements arise from the comparison, and they do not say that the treasure, the field, the pearl or the merchant represents anything.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say who the treasure or the pearl is, and it does not say what the field is. It does not say that the merchant is Christ or that the man is any group. It does not turn selling, finding, seeking or buying into steps by which a person is saved. It does not draw a meaning from another parable into these two.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The hidden treasure and the pearl are two of several parables in Matthew 13 that open with “the kingdom of heaven.” They are kept apart from their neighbors here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 13:24–30, 36–43', '“sowed good seed in his field”; “The field is the world”', 'The Wheat and the Weeds. A field appears, and Jesus names it. That naming is for that parable only and is not carried to the field of 13:44.'],
    ['Matt 13:31–33', '“a grain of mustard seed”; “leaven”', 'The Mustard Seed and the Leaven. Things that grow or work through. No finding, no selling, no buying, and no explanation from Jesus.'],
    ['Matt 13:47–50', '“a net, that was cast into the sea”', 'The Dragnet. A net gathers and a separation follows. There is no purchase here, and the furnace and wailing of 13:50 are not part of the treasure or the pearl.'],
    ['Matt 13:52', '“a man that is an householder, which bringeth forth out of his treasure things new and old”', 'The instructed scribe. The word “treasure” stands in a different sentence, about a householder and what he brings forth, and is not the hid treasure of 13:44.'],
    ['Luke 14:28–33', '“forsaketh not all that he hath”', 'The Tower and the Warring King. A different setting, a different audience and a different point (counting the cost). Its “all that he hath” is not carried into 13:44 or 13:46.'],
], [0.20, 0.30, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The treasure and the pearl come after the move into the house (13:36), in a chapter in which the other parables are told on the shore or explained in the house. The Tower and the Warring King is told on a road to great multitudes (Luke 14:25). A shared phrase such as “all that he hath” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The treasure story has a man who finds, and the pearl story has a merchant. The Wheat and the Weeds has a sower, an enemy, servants and reapers. Matthew 13:52 has a scribe and a householder. No character is carried from one parable into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The treasure and the pearl move from finding to selling to buying. The Wheat and the Weeds moves from sowing to a delayed harvest. The Dragnet moves from casting to sorting. The Mustard Seed and the Leaven move from small to large. None of those movements is read into these two.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, “the field” in 13:44 is not “the world” of 13:38. Second, “treasure” in 13:44 is not the “treasure” of 13:52. Third, “all that he hath” in 13:44 and 13:46 is not the “all that he hath” of Luke 14:33, and that verse is not an explanation of these parables.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17) and sent the Twelve to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:6). The treasure and the pearl belong to that ministry. The Kingdom they speak of is the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel, and the text places them among the things the disciples are asked about at the end of the day (13:51).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parables were told before any of that, and they are not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The “mysteries of the kingdom of heaven” that Jesus says are given to the disciples (13:11) are His own Kingdom matters given to them during His earthly ministry. They are not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul. The word is shared and the subject is not.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The treasure, the field, the merchant and the pearl are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The parables are not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. The man’s selling and the merchant’s buying are actions in two stories, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from the valuation the stories show. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of valuation.** The parables ask a reader to weigh what he holds against what is of surpassing worth, and to ask whether what he holds is rightly weighed.',
    '**A lesson of gladness.** The first man acts “for joy thereof.” A reader learns that giving up what is lesser for what is greater need not be done grudgingly.',
    '**A lesson of decisiveness.** Each man acts at once on what he has found. A reader learns that seeing the worth of something calls for a response and not for delay.',
    '**A lesson of humility.** Neither story says the man or the merchant made the treasure or the pearl. A reader learns that what is of surpassing worth is found and received, not produced.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom’s worth is not turned into the Body’s hope, and the selling of all is not turned into a requirement laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in these parables.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus gives two short comparisons. In the first, the kingdom of heaven is like treasure hid in a field that a man finds, hides, and buys the field for, selling all that he has, for joy. In the second, it is like a merchant who was seeking goodly pearls and who, when he found one pearl of great price, sold all that he had and bought it. Jesus gives no explanation of either story and names none of its parts. The comparison says that the Kingdom is of surpassing worth, that the one who finds its worth acts decisively, and that what is given up is small beside what is gained. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says what the treasure, the field, the merchant or the pearl is, or that makes the selling and the buying a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a treasure is found, hidden and bought with all the man has; a merchant seeking pearls finds one of great price and buys it with all he has.',
    '**The meaning:** the Kingdom is of surpassing worth; those who see it act decisively and gladly; what is given up is small beside what is gained.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus; no identity for the treasure, the field, the merchant or the pearl; the field of 13:38 is not the field of 13:44.',
    '**The present lesson:** valuation, gladness, decisiveness and humility, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

