A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Wheat and the Weeds, the Mustard Seed and the Leaven, the Dragnet and the Tower and the Warring King are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. These two parables make no use of their meanings, and they make none of these.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus compares the kingdom of heaven to treasure hid in a field that a man finds and buys for all he has, and to a merchant who finds one pearl of great price and sells all he has to buy it. Jesus gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that the Kingdom is of surpassing worth.'))
A(('p', 'Write the synthesis in your own words in five to seven sentences. In it:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Tell each story in its order.',
    'Say plainly which parts Jesus does not identify.',
    'State what the comparison says about the Kingdom’s worth.',
    'State when Jesus told it and what He was preaching.',
    'State one principle of conduct for today.',
    'End with one sentence naming what the parables do not say.',
]))
A(('call', 'Boundaries to Remember',
   'No explanation from Jesus, so no identity for the treasure, the field, the man, the merchant or the pearl. No symbol carried in from another parable. Selling, finding, seeking and buying are actions in stories, not steps of salvation. The Body of Christ is neither the treasure nor the pearl. The lessons are principles of conduct, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
